== Lecture 6 · Submodules, quotients, and isomorphism theorems
#focus[Construct quotient modules, identify kernels and images, and use the correspondence and isomorphism theorems.]

*Recall and approach.* Recall well-defined scalar action on additive cosets follows from submodule closure.


#definition[Submodule and quotient][A submodule $N subset.eq M$ is an additive subgroup closed under all scalars. The quotient $M slash N$ consists of additive cosets, with $r(m+N)=r m+N$. A kernel and an image of module maps are submodules.]

The scalar action on the quotient is well defined because if $m-m' in N$, then $r(m-m') in N$. This is the module analogue of normality in group quotients, but additive groups of modules are already abelian.

#strategy[Restrict or compose quotient maps and compute their kernels; use correspondence for simple modules.]

#theorem[First module isomorphism theorem][For a linear map $f:M -> P$, the induced map $M slash ker f -> "im" f$, $m+ker f |-> f(m)$, is an isomorphism.]
#proof[The additive-group theorem gives a well-defined bijective additive map. Scalar compatibility follows from $f(r m)=r f(m)$; its inverse is linear because the map is bijective and preserves scalar operations.]

#theorem[Second and third module isomorphism theorems][For submodules $A,B$ of $M$, $(A+B) slash B iso A slash(A inter B)$. For $A subset.eq B subset.eq M$, $(M slash A) slash(B slash A) iso M slash B$.]
#proof[Restrict the projection $M -> M slash B$ to $A$. Its image is $(A+B) slash B$ and its kernel is $A inter B$, proving the first result. For the second, the map $m+A |-> m+B$ is well defined because $A subset.eq B$, is surjective, and has kernel $B slash A$. Apply the first isomorphism theorem.]

Submodules of $M slash N$ correspond to submodules of $M$ containing $N$, by inverse image under the quotient map. Image followed by inverse image returns a submodule containing $N$; the reverse composite returns the original quotient submodule because the quotient map is onto. This correspondence is useful for recognizing maximal submodules and simple modules.

#definition[Simple module][A nonzero module is simple if its only submodules are zero and itself.]
#theorem[Simple modules over a commutative ring][A simple $R$-module is isomorphic to $R slash frak(m)$ for a maximal ideal $frak(m)$, and every such quotient is simple.]
#proof[A nonzero element generates a nonzero submodule, hence all of a simple module. The cyclic-module theorem identifies it with $R slash I$. Submodule correspondence says this quotient is simple exactly when no ideal lies strictly between $I$ and $R$, namely when $I$ is maximal.]

#example[An intersection calculation behind an isomorphism][In the integer module, $4ZZ+6ZZ=2ZZ$ by Bézout and $4ZZ inter 6ZZ=12ZZ$ by divisibility. The map $4ZZ->2ZZ slash 6ZZ$ has kernel $12ZZ$ and is onto: $[4]$ generates the three-element target. It gives $4ZZ slash 12ZZ iso 2ZZ slash 6ZZ$.]

This concrete example explains why the second isomorphism theorem has an intersection on one side and a sum on the other. The domain restriction creates the intersection kernel, while taking its image in the quotient creates the sum modulo the other submodule. Memorizing the formula without this map makes its direction easy to reverse.

#checkpoint("3.2", [Why is $ZZ slash 9 ZZ$ not a simple $ZZ$-module?], [It has the proper nonzero submodule generated by $[3]$, or equivalently the ideal $9 ZZ$ is not maximal.])
#takeaways[Many module proofs reduce to choosing a natural linear map and computing its kernel and image.]
